{
  "dom_id": "step-q44186",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167856/assets/832d829821a9c18af98f5c474349b09f481c225ec5b6771e0482402d44b10e38.png)\n\nGiven that the slope of the line shown above is $3,$ what is the equation of the line?",
  "worked_solution": "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ We know that the slope is $m=3,$ so we just need to work out $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the line intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,0).$ So, the $y$-intercept is $b=0.$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=3$ and the $y$-intercept $b=0$ into the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b,$ we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\ny=3x\n$$",
